teaching_blocks.content:
DataFrame.apply() applies an arbitrary function along an axis of the DataFrame, operating on each column by default, or on each row when axis=1. Writing a small function --

    def label_speed(row):
        if row["delivery_time_minutes"] > 25:
            return "slow"
        else:
            return "fast"

-- then calling `orders["speed_label"] = orders.apply(label_speed, axis=1)` runs that function once per row (because axis=1), giving it access to every column of the row at once, and collects the results into a new column.
